		continent: "africa",
		status: "red",
		legal:
			"Illegal. Same-sex acts carry a maximum penalty of life imprisonment under Section 154 of the Penal Code. In 2025, a private motion was introduced in Parliament to further tighten 'morality' laws and increase surveillance. In Zanzibar, the law is even more explicit, with Section 153 of the Zanzibar Penal Code (2004) criminalizing 'lesbianism' with up to 5 years in prison. There are no legal protections, and the 2026 climate is characterized by a continued state-led crackdown on activists.",
		travel:
			"Extreme Risk. Tanzania, including the tourist hub of Zanzibar, is high-risk for LGBTQ+ travelers. While the government occasionally assures tourists of safety, local police frequently conduct 'morality sweeps.' In 2025/2026, digital censorship has increased, with authorities monitoring dating apps to target individuals. Public displays of affection are culturally offensive and legally dangerous. Travelers are advised to use extreme discretion and avoid any behavior or accessories (including rainbow imagery) that could be seen as 'promotion.'",
		culture:
			"Hostile & Socially Conservative. A 'witch-hunt' atmosphere has persisted since 2018, exacerbated by recent political rhetoric. In 2026, the UN High Commissioner for Human Rights noted a sharp rise in violence and arbitrary detentions. Local LGBTQ+ organizations have been largely forced underground or dissolved. Society is generally unaccepting, and 'coming out' is often met with community violence or family rejection. For tourists, the 'resort bubble' offers some protection, but once outside of it, invisibility is essential.",
